# /data/config/vms.env — what every VMS unit on this box reads. On the data
# partition, never in a rootfs slot (М9 Lesson 5): an OS update must not
# change which archive this box records into.
# ================================================================================================
# NOTES — what every part of this file does and why (kept beside the code, not in a separate document)
# ================================================================================================
# # vms.env.example — `/data/config/vms.env`: what every VMS unit on this box reads
#
# **Role.** The one environment file all four Quadlet units load (`EnvironmentFile=/data/config/vms.env` in
# `vmsworker@`, `vmscontroller`, `console`, `resource`, …); this is the example to copy onto the
# box. It lives on the data partition, never in a rootfs slot (М9 Lesson 5): an OS update must not change
# which archive this box records into. `vms/__main__.py` reads every variable here;
# `test_the_image_carries_the_three_packages_and_nothing_else` checks that `PLATFORM_DIR=/data/platform
# the config store, said as a URL and nothing else (`w2cplatform.variables.open_vars`). Unset: the files
# under `$PLATFORM_DIR/config`, in-process — on a box there is no daemon, no hop and no second quorum. In a
# cluster the job sets `nomad://…`; a Kubernetes site would set its own scheme. Not one of those names
# appears in the worker, the controller or the console.
# CONFIG_URL=file:///data/platform/config
#
# What a RUNTIME must hand every process, by name, so that none of them learns which runtime it was
# (`w2cplatform/runtime.py`). Quadlet sets WORKER_NAME=%i and nothing else — on one box the hostname is
# the server and there are no labels. A Nomad jobspec fills these from NOMAD_ALLOC_INDEX,
# node.unique.name and meta.labels; a k8s manifest from the ordinal and a fieldRef.
#   SLOT_INDEX    the slot to prefer, as <prefix>-<n>; the claim is still by CAS
#   SERVER_NAME   whose resource this process writes into
#   LABELS        what this server can reach, comma-separated
#   INSTANCE_ID   this incarnation, what failover is measured from`,
# `ARCHIVE=/data/archive` and `CAPACITY=` are present.
#
# ## Notes
# - Not here, and set per unit instead: `WORKER_NAME=%i` (the slot, in `vmsworker@.container`),
#   `CONSOLE_HOST`/`CONSOLE_PORT` (in `console.container`). Not here and not needed on one box: `NOMAD_*`
#   (M11's job gives them), `CLUSTER` (the snapshot's cluster name; defaults inside the platform).
# ================================================================================================
# the platform's two stores: `<dir>/config` (FileVariables — rows, assignments, placement, epochs, slots,
# idempotency keys) and `<dir>/objects` (FsObjectStore — heartbeats, the snapshot). Every unit mounts
# `/data/platform` at the same path, the policy unit read-only. In М11 this becomes Nomad Variables and
# MinIO behind the same interfaces.
PLATFORM_DIR=/data/platform
# the resource's root: every subsystem's event buckets (`vms/<cam>/`, `rec/<recording>/`, …) and the console's
# own `console/<instance>/` marks. Also `VmsWorker.archive_root` when not passed explicitly.
ARCHIVE=/data/archive
# the server's own volume, where a recorder with nothing declared writes: a volume of ObjectStorage, opened by
# the host's obsd (`obsd.service`). Unset: `volume` BESIDE `$ARCHIVE` — `/data/volume` — and not inside the
# tree, where the resource's walks would take the ring for events and count its blocks as the tree's usage.
# ARCHIVE_VOLUME=file:///data/volume
# its size when it is first formatted — a ring: it never grows past it, and gives up its oldest minutes when
# full. Unset: four fifths of what is free, leaving two gigabytes and the disk under the watermark's low mark
# once the ring is full — of the disk the volume is on as the DAEMON sees it (`VOLUME_SPACE`), not the recorder's
# container, where /data/volume is not mounted. A volume that exists keeps its size until a declaration
# (`rec/volumes/<name>`) says another.
# ARCHIVE_QUOTA_BYTES=
# the host's ObjectStorage daemon: /run/obsd/obsd.sock, where `obsd.service` puts it — set in the recorder's own
# unit, the one process that mounts that directory. How long a recorder waits for one answer from it: shorter
# than a lease, or a silent daemon fences every recording.
# OBSD_SOCKET=/run/obsd/obsd.sock
# OBSD_TIMEOUT=10
# where `driverpack://file/<name>` resolves (`gstvms/uri.py`). Mounted read-only into the worker only.
MEDIA_DIR=/data/media
# the workers' shared-memory fan-out branch, <SHM_DIR>/<cam>.shm — a tmpfs, mounted into the worker and the
# recorder; a recorder on this box reads a camera held on this box from here instead of over RTSP.
SHM_DIR=/run/vms
# for the worker, the number of cameras it can carry (heartbeated as `capacity`, `headroom = capacity −
# assigned`); for the controller and console, the fallback used for a worker whose heartbeat has not said
# its own number. Change it per box after М9 Lesson 7's probe.
CAPACITY=50
# the scan worker's own budget (М10B Lesson 20): how many archive scans one detjob worker carries at once.
# Deliberately a different number from CAPACITY and from the detector's, and that separation is the reason
# a scan is its own subsystem: a retro-search must not be able to spend the streams live detection runs on.
SCAN_CAPACITY=2
# the survey worker's own budget (М10B Lesson 23): how many device archives one worker watches at once.
# Again a separate number, and again for the reason that decides subsystem boundaries in this course —
# watching thirty days of somebody else's NVR must not cost live detection a stream. In practice the
# binding limit is not this but the two playback sessions the device allows.
SURVEY_CAPACITY=2
# the holder's playback door (М10B Lesson 15): HTTP, because a browser has to seek what it gets and the
# recorder fetches ranges through the same door. Only cameras whose DEVICE has an archive of its own — a
# card, an NVR — answer on it; the console proxies to it for spans it did not write.
PLAYBACK_PORT=8083
# the base of the live branch's loopback RTP ports: camera <id> is sent to RTP_BASE + id. Ids are unique inside
# a cluster, so one base per box is enough — until a bench runs SEVERAL clusters on one machine (М12 Lesson 10:
# every cluster of one calls its camera 1). Then each cluster takes its own thousand.
RTP_BASE=20000
# backfill (М10B Lesson 16): how many ranges the recorder may close per pass, and the hours it may do it in,
# in LOCAL time — night is night where the camera is, not where the server is. `22-6` wraps midnight. Empty
# window: any hour. Budget 0: only what an operator asks for from the page.
BACKFILL_BUDGET=1
BACKFILL_WINDOW=22-6
# how soon written is visible (feedback CP): a sequence open longer than this is cut by the engine on a key frame,
# and a block with a finished sequence in it is written after this many seconds even if it is not full. The
# product's numbers. Smaller is sooner and more small blocks; zero leaves the engine's own (a minute for the block).
SEQUENCE_FLUSH_MS=10000
BLOCK_FLUSH_S=5
# how far behind the visible footage runs, at worst: a block that FILLS before the flush period — the block size
# over the bitrate. A scan whose interval reaches past what is recorded follows the recording until its end, and
# gives up two of these after it (`detjobworker.FOLLOW_LAGS`).
VISIBLE_LAG_SECONDS=600
# `logging.basicConfig` level in `__main__`.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
